    "millsfield:images_texts": [
      "Franchise May Be Delayed\nSePT\nSenator Quirino told H. M. Bixby this morning the franchise\nhis firm seeks may be delayed. In the photo are shown Attorney\nJames Ross, Jr., Bixby, Quirino, and Attorney Trias.\nBIXBY, QUIRINO\nDISCUSS PLAN\nAirways Franchise May Suffer\nDelay Due To Change Of\nAerial Policy\nDue to an impending change of\ngovernment policy on the granting\nof air franchises,\nno\nassurance\ncan be given that the 25-year fran-\nchise requested by the Pan-Amer-\nican Airways to operate a Hong-\nkong-Manila\nair\nroute\ncan be\ngranted immediately, H. M. Bix-\nby, vice president of the Pan Am-\nerican airways, was informed this\nmorning by Senator Eipidio Quiri-\nno, floor leader of the senate.\n(Continued on page 15)",
      "BIXBY AND QUIRINO\nDISCUSS FRANCHISE\n(Continued from page 1)\nMr. Bixby, who arrived in Ma-\nnila yesterday from Shanghai to\nurge approval of the franchise bill,\ncallled upon Senator Quirino ac-\ncompanied by\nAttorneys\nJames\nRoss, Jr., and Trias of the law\nfirm of Ross, Lawrence and Selph,\ncounseis for\nthe\nPan American\nhere.\nMr. Bixby requested that the bill\ngranting the franchise, already fil-\ned in the legislature, be passed as\nsoon as possible. The Hongkong-\nManila airline, he stated, is only\nwaiting for the approval of the\nfranchise to begin operation.\nThe groundwork on the propos-\nel airline, Mr. Bixby announced,\nhave already been completed and\ntwo airplanes, especially built for\nthe trans-oceanic\nhop, have been\nordered. These special planes are\ncapable of doing 150 miles per\nhour and will carry from 10 to 16\npassengers each. When the line is\nin operation, weekly round trips to\nHongkong will be conducted. The\nManila-Hongkong line will connect\nwith other air routes the company\nis maintaining at present in China.\nSenator Quirino, in reply, said\nthat no assurance could be given\nthat the franchise bill would be\npushed through.\nHe explained\nthat according to a suggestion of\nthe Governor General, the legisla-\nture is studying a plan to transfer\nto the department of public works\nand communications the supervi\nsion and control\nas well as the\nright to grant certificates to aerial\nlines•\nDue to this impending change of\npolicy, he said, the granting of the\nfranchise may be delayed for a\nwhile. At\npresent, according to\nlaw, no aerial line can operate in\nthe Philippines without the permis-\nsion of\nthe legislature made\nthrough a franchise bill.\nMr. Quirino, however, promised\nto consider the request carefully,\nand said that as soon as the mat-\nter of transfering the\npower to\ngrant franchises is decided, quick\naction would taken on the Pan\nAmerican franchise.\nRates for the passengers, mail\nand light valuable cargo to be car-\nried by the sea planes of the Pan-\nAmerican Airways in the proposed\nairline between Hongkong and Ma-\nnila are being studied now.\nMr. Bixby indicated that\nmost\nlikely the Pan-American will adopt\nthe schedule fixed during the in-\nternational convention in Europe\nof air companies all over the world.\nHe will confer with Director Ruiz\nof posts to get data on the amount\nof mail and the probable share of\nthe sea planes of this volume. of\nmail. The planes will also carry\nsuch light merchandise as jewelry.\nfilms, medical supplies, etc.\nNegotiations for acquiring a cer-\ntain space in San Diego island,\nLingayen, to be used as a refuel-\nling base will be conducted during\nMr. Bixby's stay here. He will also\nconfer with Captain Maughan of\nthe aeronautics division on steps\nalong lines of establishing bases\nand radio stations.",
      "i Hardly an open to\nErn saw\nthe reporter"
    ],
